course: dictionary
lesson: 13464
title: Words that paint a picture
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words show you a picture in your head. Hunched is one of them. Read this: «The old man walked slowly, hunched over his stick.» You can almost see him, with his back curved and his shoulders up. The words around it, walked slowly and over his stick, are the context clues that help you picture it.
explain: Hunched can be a describing word or a doing word. A hunched old man has a bent back. When you hunch, you bend your back and shoulders forward, often because you are cold, tired or worried. «We hunched our shoulders against the cold wind» shows you the action.
explain: Try the swap check. Put your own meaning into the sentence: «Mia sat bent forward over her book.» It still makes sense, so bent forward is a good meaning for hunched over.
fact: Hunched means bent forward with the back curved and the shoulders raised.
fact: «Hunched over» means leaning over something closely, like a book or a page.
fact: People often hunch when they are cold, tired or worried.
fact: In health lessons you learn that sitting hunched over a screen is bad for your back.
fact: Hunch, hunches, hunched and hunching are all forms of the same word.
exercise: «The old man walked slowly, hunched over his stick.» What does hunched mean here? | Running fast down the hill | Lying down to sleep | *Bent forward with his back curved | Standing very tall and proud
exercise: «Mia sat hunched over her book, reading by the lamp.» Why might Mia be sitting this way? | She is taller than everyone else | She is going to bed | She is dancing with her book | *She is leaning over her book to read closely
exercise: Your teacher says, «Do not sit hunched over your screen.» Why? | Because it makes you hungry | Because screens are too small to see | Because it makes you taller | *Because it can hurt your back
test: «We hunched our shoulders against the cold wind.» Why would you hunch your shoulders? | Because you wanted to be taller | *Because the wind was cold | Because you were dancing | Because it was sunny
test: If someone is hunched up on a bench, what might they be feeling? | *Cold, tired or worried | Very tall and proud | Happy and bouncing | Ready to run a race
exam: Which is the -ing form of hunch? | hunched | hunchy | hunches | *hunching
